====================================================================
DOMAIN: Discourse Theory of Law and Democracy (Between Facts and Norms)
SEARCH_DATE: 2026-09-25
PAPERS_FOUND: 15 total (High: 9, Medium: 5, Low: 1)
SEARCH_SOURCES: SEP, IEP, PhilPapers, Semantic Scholar, CrossRef, OpenAlex (individual lookups only), CORE (individual lookups only), NDPR, Brave web search
====================================================================

DOMAIN_OVERVIEW:
Between Facts and Norms (Faktizität und Geltung, 1992; trans. Rehg 1996) is Habermas's
mature attempt to work out the legal-political implications of the theory of
communicative action. Its central innovation is the "co-originality" (Gleichursprünglichkeit)
or "equiprimordiality" thesis: private autonomy (liberal human/civil rights) and public
autonomy (republican popular sovereignty) are not competing values to be traded off, but
mutually presuppose one another, because citizens can only be free authors of the laws
that bind them if a "system of rights" already secures the communicative liberties needed
for legitimate opinion- and will-formation. This reframes the long-standing liberal/
republican and Kant/Rousseau disputes as a discourse-theoretic reconstruction in which
legitimacy is proceduralized: law is legitimate to the extent that it could be agreed to
by all those affected in discourses meeting conditions of inclusion, publicity, and
non-coercion. Habermas terms this a "proceduralist paradigm of law," positioned against
both the liberal (formal-bourgeois) and social-welfare (materialized) paradigms.

The secondary literature clusters around three fronts identified in the search brief.
First, reception and defense/critique of co-originality: Cooke (2020) argues Habermas's
own account of private autonomy is too thin to sustain the thesis and proposes a
dialogical alternative; Rummens (2006) and Pedersen (2011) examine whether the thesis
survives comparison with Rawlsian and republican alternatives. Second, the reconstruction
of the rule of law/constitutionalism: Zurn (2002) assesses Habermas's proceduralist
account of judicial review against Ely, Perry, and Dworkin; Cronin (2006) stages a direct
Habermas-Michelman dialogue on constitutional founding, engaging the influential Michelman
"family quarrel" line of criticism from a civic-republican and constitutional-theory
perspective. Third, jurisprudential and political critique: the 1998 Rosenfeld and Arato
volume and the 2002 von Schomberg and Baynes volume assembled the first wave of
Anglophone critical responses (legal-theoretic, sociological, and normative); Baxter's
2011 monograph and 2014 reply to Wirts, Flynn, and Zurn defend a "sociological" reading
of Habermas against charges that his social theory (system/lifeworld, functional
differentiation) cannot support his normative legal theory; Ingram (2016) stages a
positivist-adjacent comparison with Kelsen; Shoikhedbrod (2020) and Hedrick (2018) press
left-critical-theory objections (respectively, from Marx's critique of legality and from
Adorno's reification thesis) that Habermas's proceduralism concedes too much to existing
liberal-constitutional orders.

RELEVANCE_TO_PROJECT:
This domain supplies the legal-political core of Habermas's political philosophy for the
review: the co-originality thesis, the "system of rights," and the proceduralist paradigm
of law are the constructs through which his discourse ethics (Domain 1) and public-sphere
theory (Domain 2) are institutionalized into a theory of constitutional democracy. The
secondary literature here is disproportionately drawn from legal theory and political
science journals (Ratio Juris, Law and Philosophy, Law and Critique, Philosophy & Social
Criticism) rather than pure philosophy venues, reflecting the interdisciplinary reception
of Between Facts and Norms.

NOTABLE_GAPS:
Michelman's own "family quarrel" essays and Rehg's translator's introduction to Between
Facts and Norms could not be independently verified as standalone, separately indexed
items through the available search tools (they surface only as citing references within
other papers' bibliographies); Rehg's co-authored 1996 Journal of Political Philosophy
article is included instead as a documented, verifiable proxy for his engagement with the
book. OpenAlex and CORE batch/keyword searches returned HTTP 429 rate-limit errors
throughout this session; individual DOI/title lookups against these APIs (used for
abstract resolution) succeeded intermittently. The following entries could not be matched
to a genuine abstract through any source and are flagged INCOMPLETE (excluded from
synthesis): rosenfeldarato1998habermaslaw, schombergbaynes2002discourse (an automated NDPR
lookup for this entry returned a review of an unrelated book on Aristotelian character
education -- a false keyword match -- and was discarded rather than kept), baxter2011habermasdiscourse,
rehgbohman1996discourse, rummens2006debate, cronin2006possibility, ingram2016morallyenlightened,
shoikhedbrod2020revolution.

SYNTHESIS_GUIDANCE:
Organize around (1) the co-originality thesis and its critics (Cooke, Rummens, Pedersen),
(2) the proceduralist reconstruction of constitutionalism and judicial review (Zurn,
Cronin, Baxter), and (3) critiques from adjacent traditions -- positivist (Ingram),
Marxist/critical-theoretic (Shoikhedbrod, Hedrick), and the broader edited-volume
critical exchanges (Rosenfeld & Arato; von Schomberg & Baynes). Note the recurring
argument, from multiple angles, that Habermas's normative co-originality claim outruns
what his own sociological premises (system/lifeworld, functional differentiation) can
support -- this is the throughline connecting Baxter, Hedrick, and Duke.

KEY_POSITIONS:
- Defenders/reconstructors of co-originality: Rehg & Bohman, Pedersen, Rummens (qualified) - 3 papers
- Immanent critics of co-originality (Habermasian but dissatisfied): Cooke, Duke, Cronin - 3 papers
- Sociological/systems-theoretic critique (theory can't support the norms): Baxter (x2), Hedrick - 3 papers
- Cross-tradition critique (positivist, Marxist): Ingram, Shoikhedbrod - 2 papers
- Foundational critical-exchange anthologies: Rosenfeld & Arato; von Schomberg & Baynes - 2 volumes
- Constitutionalism/judicial review reconstruction: Zurn - 1 paper
====================================================================
}

@book{habermas1996betweenfacts,
  author = {Habermas, Jürgen},
  title = {Between Facts and Norms: Contributions to a Discourse Theory of Law and Democracy},
  year = {1996},
  publisher = {The MIT Press},
  doi = {10.7551/mitpress/1564.001.0001},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Habermas reconstructs modern law and constitutional democracy through discourse theory, arguing that private autonomy (individual rights) and public autonomy (democratic self-legislation) are co-original: citizens can regard themselves as authors of the law that binds them as its addressees only if a "system of rights" already guarantees the communicative liberties presupposed by legitimate opinion- and will-formation. Law mediates between the "facts" of social complexity (coercive, positive enactment) and the "norms" of legitimacy (rational acceptability), and Habermas proposes a "proceduralist paradigm of law" as an alternative to liberal and welfare-state paradigms.

  RELEVANCE: This is the primary text anchoring Domain 3; nearly every secondary work in this file responds directly to its co-originality thesis, its system-of-rights argument, or its account of the proceduralist paradigm and constitutional adjudication. Translated by William Rehg.

  POSITION: Foundational primary text of discourse-theoretic legal and democratic theory.
  },
  keywords = {co-originality, system-of-rights, proceduralist-paradigm, popular-sovereignty, primary-text, High},
  abstract = {In Between Facts and Norms Jurgen Habermas works out the legal and political implications of his Theory of Communicative Action (1981), bringing to fruition the project announced with his publication of The Structural Transformation of the Public Sphere in 1962. This new work is a major contribution to recent debates on the rule of law and the possibilities of democracy in postindustrial societies, but it is much more. The introduction by William Rehg succinctly captures the special nature of the work, noting that it offers a sweeping, sociologically informed conceptualization of law and basic rights, a normative account of the rule of law and the constitutional state, an attempt to bridge normative and empirical approaches to democracy, and an account of the social context required for democracy. Finally, the work frames and caps these arguments with a bold proposal for a new paradigm of law that goes beyond the dichotomies that have afflicted modern political theory from its inception and that still underlie current controversies between so-called liberals and civic republicans. The book includes a postscript written in 1994, which restates the argument in light of its initial reception, and two appendixes, which cover key developments that preceded the book. Habermas himself was actively involved in the translation, adapting the text as necessary to make it more accessible to English-speaking readers.},
  abstract_source = {openalex}
}

