  publisher = {Stanford University Press},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Baxter provides a systematic exposition and critical assessment of Habermas's discourse theory of law and democracy, tracing it from the basic concepts of communicative action theory through Habermas's "reconstruction" of modern law, his account of adjudication, and the system/lifeworld framework of his "communication theory of society," before addressing Habermas's post-Between Facts and Norms writings on religion, multiculturalism, and the "postnational constellation."

