  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Hedrick argues Habermas's theory represents the most coherent available combination of reconciliation and reification: Habermas remains attentive to Adorno's worry that functional differentiation may undermine individual autonomy (via "colonization of the lifeworld") but resists Adorno's pessimistic conclusions by treating a democratic society's legal system as the medium through which discourse can consensually shape system/lifeworld interchanges. However, Hedrick contends Habermas achieves this synthesis only by abstracting away from the psychoanalytic concerns that occupied Adorno and Honneth, leaving his proceduralist paradigm of law ungrounded in the kind of substantive legal paradigm that would let persons identify with the system's underlying values -- yielding a democratic theory too attenuated from ordinary first-order notions of meaning and value to be genuinely emancipatory.

  RELEVANCE: Provides a Frankfurt-School-internal critique of the proceduralist paradigm of law, arguing that Habermas's discourse-theoretic reconstruction of law purchases its critical-theoretic credentials (avoiding Adorno's pessimism) at the cost of an overly formal, identification-poor conception of legal legitimacy.

  POSITION: Internal critical-theory critic; situates Habermas between Adorno's reification thesis and a more substantive, recognition-based account of legal identification.
